\paragraph{Designed against random selection.}
Table~\ref{tab:primary_contrast} gives the pre-registered contrast. Designed selection beats random selection with the interval beyond $\delta$ at every $k\le80$, with a gain of 1.6 points at $k=1$, 1.9 at $k=2$ and 3, 0.8 at 8 and 0.5 at 20, and for 86--96\% of held-out models at $k\le20$. At the secondary margin the gain is practically relevant only for $k\le3$: at $k=5$ the verdict is undecided and from $k=8$ random selection is equivalent. Design thus helps statistically almost throughout and practically only when very few benchmarks can be run. The other rules do less. The most-observed benchmarks (S1) are worse than random selection at every $k\le109$ (by 1.3 to 4.0 points at $k\le8$); the highest-variance benchmarks (S2) beat it at $k=1$ (1.3 points [0.8, 1.8]) and are within $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ of it from $k=2$; pivoted QR (S4) helps at $k\le2$ and is worse by $k=5$. Choosing the run set knowing the target set (S6 with $T$ as target) changes the gain by at most 0.05 points, and a single-swap search from the greedy set (S6s) by at most 0.22. The greedy set is not the best of its own criterion when $k$ is small: on 24 models of one fold the exhaustive optimum has a criterion 18\% and 35\% lower at $k=2$ and 3 and an RMSE lower by 1.1 and 0.5 points, and the gap is 5\% at $k=8$ and 2\% at $k=20$ with no gain in error (Appendix~\ref{app:further}), so the gains at $k\le3$ are, if anything, understated.
Three further baselines (a cross-validated greedy wrapper S3, $k$-medoids S5 and a tree-based interview in the style of \citet{golbandi2011adaptive}, S7; 20 splits, Appendix~\ref{app:further}) help at the pre-set margin only at $k\le8$, $k\le20$ and $k\le3$, and S3 and S7 are worse than random selection from $k=20$ and 12; on synthetic data from the model S7 is also behind S6 at every $k\ge2$ (Appendix~\ref{app:checks}). Only the factor model's own criterion stays ahead of random selection over the whole range.

\paragraph{Inference rules.}
With S6 fixed, we replace the factor-model posterior mean by ridge regression on the revealed benchmarks (P2), $k$ nearest training models (P3), a Soft-Impute row update (P4~\citep{mazumder2010spectral}) and principal-component regression with a sigmoid link (P6, an adaptation of observational scaling laws~\citep{ruan2024observational}, not as published), on the same 154 models and 20 splits (Table~\ref{tab:pipelines}, Appendix~\ref{app:further}). None beats the factor model by more than the secondary margin at any $k$. The nearest-neighbour rule is better at $k\le2$ (by 1.04 points [0.50, 1.57] at $k=1$) and worse from $k=5$; ridge regression is worse at $k\le8$ and better by 0.15 to 0.29 points at $k=20$ and 32; P1 and P4 are worse. Which rule is best depends on $k$.

\paragraph{The error is mostly floor.}
Figure~\ref{fig:theory_vs_measured}c separates the error of Section~\ref{sec:theory} into its terms. At $k=20$ the mean squared error of the headline subset is 45.7 points$^2$ [39.9, 52.1], of which the predicted noise accounts for 11.8, the design term for 20.7, the remainder for $-4.7$ [$-8.2$, $-0.9$] and the floor misfit (the floor's excess over its predicted noise) for 17.9 [12.3, 24.6], which is the same at every $k$. The design term and the gain it predicts are close to what we measure (the predicted gain of S6 over S0 is at most 0.36 points above and 0.03 below the measured gain at every $k\le20$), and the predicted $k^*$ is on the safe side of the empirical one (Table~\ref{tab:primary_contrast}; 80 against 32 at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$, 20 against 5 at $2\delta_{\text{floor}}$). The floor is where the model fails: the measured floor MSE is 29.7 points$^2$, 2.5 times the 11.8 the fitted factor model predicts, and the ratio is larger on the strength and temporal splits (7.9 and 9.6; 22 and 13 models). It does not come from the rank: the floor is 5.06 points at the selected rank, 4.89 at rank 24 and 4.76 at each model's own best rank (an optimistic reference). About half of it (55\%) is across-organisation extrapolation, since the ratio falls from 2.5 to 1.7 when the held-out model's organisation is left in the training set.

Synthetic data from the model (Figure~\ref{fig:theory_vs_measured}a--b) show what to expect if it were right. The estimation error is small and of order $1/M$ (slope $-1.2$ in $\log M$; Figure~\ref{fig:estimation}, Appendix~\ref{app:checks}), and the shape bound of Section~\ref{sec:theory} holds in both regimes: with six separated factors the error falls from 0.58 at $k=3$ to 0.21 at $k=8$ and is flat after, with a decaying spectrum it falls gradually (0.60, 0.49, 0.44 at $k=3$, 8, 20). Four kinds of misspecification (a saturating link, heavy-tailed noise, noise that shrinks near a ceiling, residuals correlated within a task type) leave the measured error 11--17\% above the prediction at $k=20$, against 29\% on the real matrix (a qualitative comparison only: the synthetic target is all benchmarks not run, in standardised units, with no separate floor term). The real matrix lies outside every single rung on the diagnostics fixed before looking at it (calibration of the predictive variance 2.8 against 1.1--1.3; excess kurtosis of the residuals 29 against at most 4, or 102 for the heavy-tailed rung) and outside the combination of three rungs fixed in advance (1.2 and 4.2), as do the other four held-out settings (Appendix~\ref{app:checks}). We do not know what produces the missing factor 2.5, and we do not offer the risk as a rule that gives $k$ from the spectrum: it attributes the design part of the error, and the floor is measured.

