\paragraph{A second matrix.}
On the Open LLM Leaderboard (Table~\ref{tab:olb} in Appendix~\ref{app:further}; $N=39$, $k\le13$) the small-$k$ finding carries over. The gap between adjacent models is zero on this matrix, so the margin fixed in advance is $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ (about 1.1 points): designed selection helps at it only for $k\le3$ on held-out families and $k\le5$ on the strongest quarter, and random selection is equivalent from $k=5$ and $k=8$. Against a margin of zero the interval is above zero at every $k\le13$, by 2.1 and 2.7 points at $k=1$ and 1.4 and 2.5 at $k=3$. The floors are 4.8 and 7.1 points. The excess over the floor is at most 2 points from $k=2$ on families and $k=3$ on the strongest quarter and at most 1 point from $k=5$ and $k=13$; the $k$ needed for a given excess in points differs between the matrices (2 points: 8 benchmarks on MTEB, 2 here). The risk of Section~\ref{sec:theory} does better here than on MTEB for held-out families: the measured floor MSE is 1.08 times the factor model's predicted noise (2.5 on MTEB), the predicted gain of S6 over S0 (2.4, 2.1, 1.5, 0.8, 0.5 and 0.3 points at $k=1,2,3,5,8,13$) is close to the measured one (2.1, 1.8, 1.4, 0.8, 0.4, 0.3), and the predicted $k^*$ at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ is 5 against 3 measured. On the strongest quarter, as on the strength and temporal splits of MTEB, it does not: the floor is 2.5 times the predicted noise and the predicted gain is about half the measured one at $k=2$ (1.6 against 3.3 points). The factor 2.5 is therefore not a property of benchmarks in general: it appears under a shift in strength on both matrices and under group hold-out on MTEB only.

\paragraph{Reading $k^*$ from the training models.}
Table~\ref{tab:ksrule} (Appendix~\ref{app:further}) compares the rule of Section~\ref{sec:method} with the measured $k^*$. On held-out organisations of MTEB, $\hat k$ equals the measured $k^*$ at 7 of 8 margins (0.25 to 3 points and the two floor margins) and is within one grid step at all 8, and its interval contains the measured value at all 8. On the leaderboard the rule was fixed in advance, with the criterion of one grid step at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ and $2\delta_{\text{floor}}$ on both subsets: it meets it for held-out families, equalling the measured $k^*$ at all 7 margins the grid reaches (3 against 3 at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$; 0.25 point is not shown to be reached by 13 benchmarks). Under a shift it fails: on the temporal split of MTEB it overstates $k^*$ by 2 to 6 grid steps at every margin (13 models); on the strength split it errs by up to 2 steps either way, with the measured value inside its interval at 5 of 8 margins (22 models); and on the strongest quarter of the leaderboard it understates it by 1 to 4 steps, which fails the criterion at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ (2 against 13) and meets it at twice that. It is a usable estimate for a new model that resembles those in the matrix, and not claimed beyond that.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Figure~\ref{fig:scope} (Appendix~\ref{app:further}) varies one condition at a time on four of the ten held-out folds (67 models, 23 organisations, 20 splits; 68 and 75 models for the task types and the 75-task suite). Less data raises the floor, so we give $k^*$ at 0.25 of each condition's own floor. With all training data the floor is 5.3 points and $k^*$ is 32; with half of the training models 5.8 and 20, with a quarter 6.8 and 5; with 70 and 30\% of the training cells 6.0 and 7.0 and 8 and 3. Predicting from the logit of the scores gives 20 and the 75-task suite 5; with the ten task types as the benchmarks ($N=10$, floor 4.7) it gives 2 [1, 3], a share of 0.20 against 0.10 at $N=328$ and 0.07 for the 75 tasks, so coarser benchmarks need a larger share of them. Thinner data raises the error at every $k$ rather than the number of benchmarks needed. In every condition the gain of designed over random selection at $k=20$ is 4--8\% of the floor.
